---
import "../../styles/s03g8/S03_Group8_physics.css";
---

<section class="physics-wall-section" aria-labelledby="physics-wall-title">
    <!-- MAIN: THE PHYSICS WALL CARD -->
    <div class="physics-wall-card">
        <div class="physics-wall-card-header">
            <h2 id="physics-wall-title" class="physics-wall-title">
                THE PHYSICS WALL
            </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="physics-wall-content">
            <!-- LEFT: EXPLANATION TEXT -->
            <article class="physics-wall-copy">
                <span class="physics-wall-alert">CRITICAL LIMIT REACHED</span>

                <h3 class="physics-wall-topic">
                    800mm² Reticle Limit &amp; Defect Density
                </h3>

                <p class="physics-wall-description">
                    Moore's Law demanded more transistors, but the lithography
                    machines hit a hard physical ceiling: the 800mm² reticle
                    limit. Lenses could only project light onto an area so
                    large. As monolithic dies grew to fill this entire printable
                    space, they encountered a devastating statistical reality:
                    defect density.
                </p>

                <p class="physics-wall-description">
                    In a cleanroom, a single microscopic speck of dust is
                    catastrophic. When your entire chip relies on one massive
                    piece of silicon, one defect destroys the entire unit,
                    destroying yield rates and rendering the manufacturing
                    process unprofitable. Today's most advanced nodes push
                    this even further: public estimates place a leading-edge
                    300mm wafer at about $17,000, meaning a failed
                    near-reticle-class chip can waste far more than the
                    $10,000+ figures seen on older process nodes.
                </p>

                <p class="physics-wall-description">
                    The 800mm² ceiling comes from the projection optics in
                    ASML's lithography systems. The lens can only expose a fixed
                    field size onto the wafer in a single pass, roughly 26 ×
                    33mm. Any die that exceeds this reticle area cannot be
                    printed without stitching multiple exposures, a process so
                    costly and error-prone that it is impractical for volume
                    production.
                </p>

                <p class="physics-wall-description">
                    The financial stakes were already enormous back in 2015.
                    At the 16nm node, a single 300mm wafer cost approximately
                    $8,000 to process. A monolithic die approaching the
                    reticle limit yielded so few good parts that each
                    scrapped die wasted thousands of dollars in silicon, fab
                    time, and lost revenue, and as the paragraph above
                    shows, that problem has only gotten more expensive on
                    newer nodes.
                </p>

                <cite class="physics-wall-cite">
                    Sources: ASML lithography field-size specifications;
                    industry 300mm wafer cost estimates (2019–2023).
                </cite>

                <p class="physics-wall-transition">
                    These constraints drove AMD to abandon the monolithic
                    approach entirely, the solution was to stop building one
                    large die and start composing many small ones.
                </p>
            </article>

            <!-- RIGHT: MONOLITHIC FAILURE VISUAL -->
            <figure class="physics-wall-monitor">
                <div class="physics-wall-monitor-header">
                    <span class="physics-wall-monitor-code">
                        SIM: MONOLITHIC_FAILURE
                    </span>
                    <span class="physics-wall-monitor-status">
                        STATUS: CRITICAL
                    </span>
                </div>

                <!-- Reticle wrapper: outer = 800mm² limit, inner = actual die -->
                <div class="physics-wall-reticle">
                    <span class="physics-wall-reticle-label"
                        >800mm² RETICLE LIMIT</span
                    >

                    <div class="physics-wall-die">
                        <span class="physics-wall-defect-dot"></span>
                        <span class="physics-wall-defect-text">DEFECT</span>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <figcaption class="physics-wall-monitor-footer">
                    <span class="physics-wall-yield">YIELD: 0%</span>
                    <span class="physics-wall-cost-note"
                        >2015: $8,000 WAFER → $0 RETURNED</span
                    >
                    <span class="physics-wall-failure-note">
                        1 PARTICLE = 1 DEAD DIE
                    </span>
                </figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>